## Report
Rubric-driven steps: attempted dynamic reproduction first. Ran `cargo test -p server-core race_duplicate_segment_cleanup_can_delete_committed_shards -- --nocapture` (PoC added) but build failed because ec-sys requires libisal: build.rs panicked with “libisal not found; install intel-isa-l or set PKG_CONFIG_PATH” (cargo output). Valgrind and gdb are not installed in the container (`valgrind --version` and `gdb --version` both not found), so crash/valgrind/debugger validation could not proceed. 

Code analysis confirms the TOCTOU race: append_stream_segment performs an initial duplicate check under the metadata PG lock, then releases the lock and writes shard files without holding any PG mutex (comment at coordinator.rs:3643-3649) via write_stream_segment_shards (coordinator.rs:3515-3615). After writing, it reacquires locks and repeats the duplicate check (coordinator.rs:3734-3738); on duplicate, it calls cleanup_stream_shards_locked (coordinator.rs:3618-3621), which invokes PgStore::delete_shard to unlink shard files and delete their SQLite records (pg_store.rs:663-700). Shard keys are derived solely from session_id + segment_index via stream_segment_key_hash (pg.rs:112-121, used at coordinator.rs:3672-3678), so concurrent uploads of the same session/segment share identical shard keys. Thus, the losing request’s cleanup can delete shards already registered by the winner, leaving committed metadata pointing to missing shards and causing data loss/corruption.

